  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The synthesis relies on a heterogeneous pool of existing studies with varying designs and measures, which may limit direct comparability.",
      "what": "This study synthesized 124 empirical evaluations of cycling interventions using a rule-based classification framework to compare outcomes across diverse study designs. It analyzed interventions by type, cost, and behavioral mechanisms, assessing the direction, strength, and confidence of their impacts on cycling behavior.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that structural changes like bike paths often merely redistribute existing riders rather than recruit new ones, and that behavioral campaigns rarely sustain change alone. Instead, interventions that directly improve practical access, such as e-bike subsidies and bike-share schemes, are the most consistent drivers of new cycling uptake."
